-## Our goals as of July 10th, 2026
+- October 19, 2026: “Moonshot R&D” projects. We believe we broadly achieved the substantive components of the first goal. We created a prototype of what our key workflows and infrastructure would look like if they were subject to extreme security practices. We’ve explored the longest lead-time systems that needed to change to achieve air-gapped operations. Most of our effort has been spent building prototype environments in which we run workloads to test what productivity cost they’ll incur and did not focus on commensurate physical security controls, in favor of other work. We plan to deploy inference systems in isolated environments in the near future. We also created an inventory of the needed controls and as the next step, have begun putting them into production in our environments. We believe we broadly achieved the second goal in full. For the provable inference project, we built a prototype that runs on production inference and checks that outputs come from a specific set of model weights. In developing this prototype, we rolled out signing on all of our internal token traffic, which has already caught one extremely rare, benign integrity glitch. We now use the term “inference verification,” rather than “provable inference”, our original aim with this moonshot is to achieve high assurance in the integrity of our token stream, which can be achieved in several ways, and “provable inference” suggests a narrower approach than we believe is necessary.
+
+- October 19, 2026: Leveling up across the board. We added goals in two areas that matter more as our own datacenter buildout and bare-metal deployments ramp up: datacenter physical security and platform security.
+
+- October 19, 2026: World-class internal red-teaming. We completed this goal ahead of its January 1, 2027 target. We built an automated red-teaming system and compared it with the bug bounties we ran against our biological weapons classifiers between April 20 and September 11, 2026. On the same 8 bounty questions and 3 generations of production classifiers, it matched or beat the best human submission in all 24 question-classifier pairs, winning 23. It did this in 24 to 48 hours, compared with weeks to months of bounty time. We judge these findings to be more substantial in aggregate than our bug bounty findings, which is the test we set. We plan to publish a more in-depth blog post on this in the future. We removed this goal from the roadmap, as we have completed it.
+
+- October 19, 2026: Upholding Claude’s Constitution. We have kept our Constitution up to date and public. Our automated alignment assessment evaluates alignment with the Constitution as a core metric, and this is reported for all assessed models. Interpretability methods have become an important part of our overall alignment assessment methodology, and evidence produced using interpretability is featured in all recent system cards. We have red-teamed our alignment arguments using model organisms and published the findings in Risk Reports, though not as extensively as we had intended when drafting this goal. We initially ran an oversight process over a representative sample of our post-training data and rewards to check alignment with the Constitution, but found that it surfaced nothing beyond what our existing review processes were already catching, so we no longer run it routinely. A recent re-run on a sample of training data again flagged nothing. We are retiring this goal in its current form while we spend more time reflecting on which goals are most effective for alignment, and we will publish more about our thinking in the future. At a minimum, we plan to continue to update the public Constitution to match the most recent trained-on Constitution within 90 days of relevant internal or external deployments of models trained on updated Constitutions.
+
+- October 19, 2026: New policy goals. We added two new policy goals. These are a policy proposal on embedded third-party evaluators (due December 15, 2026) and a policy framework for targeting the pace of AI development (due January 15, 2027).
+
